"Jacek Dziedzic" <jacek@janowo.net> wrote:

> Hi!

>     I'm into nethack for a week now, so my questions might seem silly,
> still I hope some of you will give me some (spoily) help.

Okay, but lots of us have played NetHack for many years without ever
ascending; "spoilers" can spoil most of the fun of the game, which is
partly figuring stuff out on your own, and partly avoiding gaining, or
at least somehow losing, horrid habits of impatience and carelessness.
In terms of self-control, NetHack is probably the hardest game you will
ever play.  By design, almost everything you can do in haste or without
thinking provides a risk of killing you on the spot.

>     First of all, I'm getting frustrated about how quickly my characters
> get hungry and how hard it is to find food. Things like pears, pancakes
> or food rations seem like a blessing to me, for I often find myself eating
> tripe rations or corpses, which I guess is not too good. I also try to #pray
> when hungry. Is eating things that "taste terrible" any good (like do they
> reduce hunger despite tasting bad)?

Yeah, that's okay, but the ones that "must have been poisonous" aren't too
good for you, they cream both your hit points and do a long term (but
"temporary") reduction of your strength, which is _really_ annoying, since
you can't carry as much as fast, then.

>     Second, and most frustrating is my inability to explore the dungeon,
> even on DL1 or DL2. I try to (s)earch, sometimes multiple times, but
> this wastes so much time that I usually have to worry about finding food
> quickly.

As others have said, you are trying to explore more than is easily accessible.
It is a part of learning the game to learn to balance the cost of searching
obsessively, against the cost of missing parts of a level.  For myself, I
absolutely _never_ waste time searching for traps, just take the bad luck as
it comes (but see above about never ascending before assuming this is wise).

You might want to start learning with a Ranger role, they have both long
range weapons that keep them out of melee combat very often, and a very high
searching skill, which you'll love.  Mostly just walking by a hidden door, a
ranger sniffs it out somehow.

Another good idea is to explore all the parts you _can_ visit before doing
any searching _at all_.  Sometimes you can get to a room by a round-about path
that comes in through a doorway which is _not_ hidden, and since you'll need
to search tens of times to find some one bit of hidden stuff, backtracking and
going around is often much faster and easier on your appetite.

It also helps a lot to know that a substantial fraction of dungeon digging is
unsuccessful, for reasons known only to the RNG (and those of us who've
analyzed that part of the source code), so many paths that you find an end
of that hasn't gone anywhere, _don't_ go anywhere, they just die out there in
stone blind space somewhere, and searching is just another seductive way to
die early and often.

>     Also, are gems of any value, except for writing Elbereth with them
> or firing them from a sling?

Oh, yeah, they have _lots_ of interesting uses.  This is one of the ones most
fun to learn over time.

>     Fourth, and last question, is #sitting any good except on a throne?

Yep, but there are also places where _not_ sitting is just a dandy idea, so
watch where you plant your tush.

